Description: Fire change sensitivities on continuously assigned nets at time 0
 A net driven by an undelayed continuous assignment takes its driver's value
 at time 0 (IEEE 1800-2023 4.9.1, 6.6): in a four-state simulator such as
 xsim, that is a change from z/x that wakes `always @(net)`. With --timing,
 Verilator does not fire it (since 5a410e95a, #8911), except for nets driven
 by a constant (61ff77472). Xilinx XPM models depend on it: xpm_fifo_axis in
 packet mode with independent clocks keeps the packet count's carry in an
 `always @(axis_rd_eop)` that must run at time 0, and without it presents
 incomplete packets. Fire a change sensitivity at time 0 when it references a
 variable driven by an undelayed continuous assignment (or a port
 connection, 4.9.6), as for constant nets: not edge sensitivities, nor
 variables with declaration initializers (6.8), nor delayed assignments.
 Test: t_sched_sense_net_init{,_timing}.
Forwarded: no (upstream chose otherwise in #8911; carried for nvcx, which
 runs Verilator cores alongside xsim)
Author: Graeme Smecher <gsmecher@t0.technology>
Last-Update: 2026-11-07
---
